<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Olaf Tryggvason</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 963–<a href="/years/1000">1000</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as Olaf I of Norway</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Olaf Tryggvason was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> from 995 to 1000. A great-grandson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harald-fairhair">Harald Fairhair</a> according to saga tradition, he gained wealth as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> leader in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and was baptised before seizing power in Norway. He forced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> on Norway and sent missionaries to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/faroe-islands">Faroes</a>, Iceland and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greenland">Greenland</a>, and traditionally founded Nidaros, now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trondheim">Trondheim</a>. He was killed at the naval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-svolder">Battle of Svolder</a> in 1000.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/olaf-tryggvason.jpg" alt="Olaf Tryggvason" width="320" height="150" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ole Andreas Øverland (1855-1911) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Olav_Tryggvasson_mynt.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Haakon the Good, raised at the court of Æthelstan of England, ruled in the mid-tenth century; <b>Olaf Tryggvason</b> (r. 995–1000) forced conversion on the coastal districts until his death at the Battle of Svolder; and Olaf Haraldsson (r. 1015–1028) completed the work of Christianisation.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-norway">Kingdom of Norway · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In Norway, the kings <b>Olaf Tryggvason</b>, at the end of the 10th century, and Olaf Haraldsson, killed at the battle of Stiklestad in 1030 and soon venerated as a saint, imposed Christianity, often by force.</blockquote><a href="/religion/norse-religion">Norse religion · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>In Norway <b>Olaf Tryggvason</b>, king from 995 to 1000, and Olaf Haraldsson, killed at Stiklestad in 1030 and soon venerated as Saint Olaf, imposed the new faith by force; Iceland’s Althing accepted it by agreement in 999 or 1000; and Sweden converted more gradually, into the 12th century.</blockquote><a href="/culture/vikings">Vikings · Beliefs &amp; Worldview</a></li><li><blockquote>No throne was safe: Harald Bluetooth was overthrown by his own son, Sweyn Forkbeard, and <b>Olaf Tryggvason</b> was killed in battle in 1000.</blockquote><a href="/societies/vikings">Social Hierarchy of the Vikings</a></li><li><blockquote>In a large naval battle in the western Baltic, King <b>Olaf Tryggvason</b> of Norway was defeated and killed by an alliance of Sweyn Forkbeard of Denmark, Olof Skötkonung of Sweden and Eric Haakonsson, Earl of Lade.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/september-9">9 September in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The Færeyinga saga tells how Sigmundur Brestisson brought Christianity around 999 for King <b>Olaf Tryggvason</b>, opposed by the chieftain Tróndur í Gøtu.</blockquote><a href="/state/faroese-commonwealth">Faroese Commonwealth</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-svolder">Battle of Svolder</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=59.93,10.68&amp;year=987" aria-label="Norway on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4774 155 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Olaf Tryggvason"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5224 425h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5224 425h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=59.93,10.68&amp;year=987">What was here in 987 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Strike the sails; never shall men of mine think of flight. I never fled from battle. Let God dispose of my life, but flight I shall never take.&#8221;</p><footer>Words before the battle of Svolder, 1000 (Snorri Sturluson, Heimskringla) &#183; tr. Samuel Laing, 1844 &#183; Snorri's saga, written about 1230 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/598/pg598-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;He, methinks, has good reason for meeting us; and we may expect the sharpest conflict with these men, for they are Norsemen like ourselves.&#8221;</p><footer>Words at the battle of Svolder, 1000 (Snorri Sturluson, Heimskringla, Saga of Olaf Trygvason) &#183; tr. Samuel Laing, 1844 &#183; Snorri Sturluson's account, written two centuries later; on seeing the ships of Earl Eirik &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/598/pg598-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Why do ye strike so gently that ye seldom cut?&#8221;</p><footer>Words at the battle of Svolder, 1000 (Snorri Sturluson, Heimskringla, Saga of Olaf Trygvason) &#183; tr. Samuel Laing, 1844 &#183; Snorri Sturluson's account, written two centuries later &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/598/pg598-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 539 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
